# Collection Iterator Standard

Every APAS-VERUS collection module implements the verus 0.2026.09.13 prophetic iterator model. The verified reference implementations are the standards:

- `src/standards/iterators_standard.rs` — delegated style (a Vec-backed collection returns the std iterator), with the loop idioms.
- `src/standards/prophetic_iterators_standard.rs` — delegated and custom styles (a custom iterator implements `IteratorSpecImpl`).
- `src/standards/wrapping_iterators_standard.rs` — a collection that wraps another re-exposes the inner iterator.

Prose reference: `docs/PropheticIterators.md`. Component checklist: `docs/APAS-VERUSIterators.rs`. Measurements: `docs/StandardsUpgrade.md`.

## Required Components (all inside verus!)

Delegated style, for a collection whose elements live in a `Vec<T>`. vstd already supplies `IteratorSpecImpl` for `std::slice::Iter` and `std::vec::IntoIter`, so the collection defines no iterator struct, no iterator `View` and no ghost iterator.

| # | Component | Section |
|---|-----------|---------|
| 1 | `fn iter(&self) -> std::slice::Iter<'_, T>` with the three postconditions below | 8 (trait), 9 (impl) |
| 2 | `impl IntoIterator for &Self`, same type, same postconditions | 10 |
| 3 | Optional: `impl IntoIterator for Self -> std::vec::IntoIter<T>` | 10 |

Custom style, for a collection with no slice underneath: the iterator struct (private fields, `#[verifier::type_invariant]`), a closed `elts()` spec fn, `impl Iterator` with a plain `next` (no `ensures`), `impl IteratorSpecImpl` with the five spec fns (`obeys_prophetic_iter_laws`, `remaining`, `will_return_none`, `decrease`, `peek`), and a constructor with `#[verifier::when_used_as_spec]`.

## Constructor postconditions

```rust
fn iter(&self) -> (it: std::slice::Iter<'_, T>)
    ensures
        IteratorSpec::remaining(&it) == self@.as_ref(),
        vstd::std_specs::slice::into_iter_elts(it) == self@,
        IteratorSpec::decrease(&it) is Some,
```

For a consuming iterator drop `.as_ref()` and use `vstd::std_specs::vec::into_iter_elts`. For a custom type the middle clause is `IteratorSpec::remaining(&it) == it.elts()`.

Rule for trait methods returning a same-crate iterator type (an adaptor or a custom type): state clauses 1 and 3 over the inner std iterator (`&it.inner`), not over the returned type, or the returned type's `next` fails verification on 09.13. Inherent methods and free functions may name the returned type.

## Proof-Time Test (PTT) Standard

Every collection with an iterator has a PTT file at `rust_verify_test/tests/<Chap>/Prove<Collection>.rs`, registered in `rust_verify_test/Cargo.toml`.

### Required test patterns

| # | Pattern | Creates iterator via | Loop style | Yields |
|---|---------|---------------------|------------|--------|
| 1 | loop-borrow-iter | `let mut it = a.iter();` | `loop { match it.next() }` | `&T` |
| 2 | loop-borrow-into | `let mut it = (&a).into_iter();` | `loop { match it.next() }` | `&T` |
| 3 | for-borrow-iter | `a.iter()` | `for x in it: a.iter()` | `&T` |
| 4 | for-borrow-into | `(&a).into_iter()` | `for x in it: (&a).into_iter()` | `&T` |
| 5 | loop-consume | `let mut it = a.into_iter();` | `loop { match it.next() }` | `T` |
| 6 | for-consume | `a.into_iter()` | `for x in it: a.into_iter()` | `T` |

Patterns 5 and 6 apply only to collections with `IntoIterator for Self`.

### Loop pattern template (borrow)

```rust
test_verify_one_file! {
    #[test] collection_loop_borrow_iter verus_code! {
        use vstd::prelude::*;
        use vstd::std_specs::iter::*;
        use apas_verus::<Chap>::<Module>::<Module>::*;

        fn test_loop_borrow_iter() {
            let a: CollectionS<u64> = /* construct */;
            let ghost orig: Seq<u64> = a@;
            let mut collected: Vec<u64> = Vec::new();
            let mut it: std::slice::Iter<'_, u64> = a.iter();
            let ghost mut pos: int = 0;
            loop
                invariant
                    IteratorSpec::obeys_prophetic_iter_laws(&it),
                    IteratorSpec::decrease(&it) is Some,
                    0 <= pos <= orig.len(),
                    IteratorSpec::remaining(&it).len() == orig.len() - pos,
                    forall|i: int| 0 <= i < IteratorSpec::remaining(&it).len()
                        ==> *(#[trigger] IteratorSpec::remaining(&it)[i]) == orig[pos + i],
                    collected.len() == pos,
                    forall|i: int| 0 <= i < collected.len()
                        ==> #[trigger] collected@[i] == orig[i],
                decreases IteratorSpec::decrease(&it)->0,
            {
                let ghost old_pos = pos;
                match it.next() {
                    Some(x) => {
                        proof {
                            pos = pos + 1;
                            assert(orig[old_pos] == *x);
                        }
                        collected.push(*x);
                    },
                    None => {
                        assert(pos == orig.len());
                        assert(collected@ =~= orig);
                        break;
                    },
                }
            }
        }
    } => Ok(())
}
```

### For pattern template (borrow)

```rust
test_verify_one_file! {
    #[test] collection_for_borrow_iter verus_code! {
        use vstd::prelude::*;
        use vstd::std_specs::iter::*;
        use apas_verus::<Chap>::<Module>::<Module>::*;

        fn test_for_borrow_iter() {
            let a: CollectionS<u64> = /* construct */;
            let ghost orig: Seq<u64> = a@;
            let mut collected: Vec<u64> = Vec::new();
            for x in it: a.iter()
                invariant
                    it.seq() == orig.as_ref(),
                    collected.len() == it.index(),
                    forall|i: int| 0 <= i < collected.len()
                        ==> #[trigger] collected@[i] == *it.seq()[i],
            {
                collected.push(*x);
            }
            assert(collected@ =~= orig);
        }
    } => Ok(())
}
```

### Consuming variants

In consuming tests (`loop-consume`, `for-consume`): use `a.into_iter()`, annotate `std::vec::IntoIter<u64>`, write `it.seq() == orig` (no `.as_ref()`), no deref (`it.seq()[i]` in a `for` loop; `IteratorSpec::remaining(&it)[i] == orig[pos + i]` and `orig[old_pos] == x` in a manual loop), and push `x` rather than `*x`.

A manual loop never uses `VerusForLoopWrapper`: vstd declares `std_specs` only under `verus_keep_ghost`, so a wrapper loop does not compile under `cargo` (`src/experiments/prophetic_manual_loop_next.rs`, r211).

## Key differences: loop vs for

| Aspect | loop pattern | for pattern |
|--------|-------------|-------------|
| Syntax | `let mut it = expr;` + `loop { match it.next() }` | `for x in it: expr { ... }` |
| Ghost state | `let ghost mut pos: int` (items consumed), `IteratorSpec::remaining(&it)` | `it.index()`, `it.seq()`, `it.history()` on the named wrapper |
| Extra invariants | `obeys_prophetic_iter_laws(&it)`, `decrease(&it) is Some`, `0 <= pos <= orig.len()`, the two `remaining` clauses | none |
| Termination | explicit `decreases IteratorSpec::decrease(&it)->0` | automatic from `decrease is Some` |
| Postcondition | `assert` before `break` | `assert` after the loop; `it` out of scope |

`it.seq()` is prophetic and may not appear in `decreases`.

## Building the verus library for PTTs

`scripts/ptt.sh` compiles the library and runs every PTT; `scripts/ptt.sh <filter>` runs a case-insensitive subset. It compiles `src/lib.rs` with `--compile -o target/verus/libapas_verus.rlib --export target/verus/apas_verus.vir`, then runs `cargo nextest` in `rust_verify_test/`.
